  "description": "Hundreds of hands stencilled in red, white, black and ochre on the wall of a cave above the Río Pinturas in Patagonia. The painters held a hand to the rock and blew pigment over it through a bone pipe; most are left hands, so the pipe was held in the right. Guanaco hunts and geometric patterns were painted around them over several thousand years.",
  "facts": [
   "The oldest layer has been dated to around 9,300 years ago.",
   "Of about 830 hands, only 31 are right hands.",
   "The cave was listed as a World Heritage Site in 1999."
